From: macos15-guest-stack <kirin@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] pools: cap the DEVICE_LOCAL image slab with a heap-derived budget

The registry's live-resident population is bounded by
ResourcePools::reclaim_for_allocation_retry, which only runs when
vkAllocateMemory returns VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY.  On the Maleoon 920 / hvgr
host that bound never fires: the kernel logs GPU_OVER_LIMIT past ~6 GiB of
process GPU pages but keeps handing out memory, so a mostly idle desktop
grew the registry to 7.2 GiB / 2 688 residents - half of them evictable by
the engine's own predicate - until the guest panicked behind
never-signalling fences.

reclaim_over_slab_budget() moves the same lawful reclaim
(recoverable_residents: not pinned, not sole copy, not window-held) ahead
of the kernel's wall instead of behind a driver error that never arrives.
It runs on the resident-admission miss path of registry_ensure, the site
the out-of-memory arm already reclaims from, and trims the recycle pools
for the reason that arm does.  The budget itself is a quarter of the
device's local heap, floored at 256 MiB, narrowable (never widenable)
through REIMS_VGPU_SLAB_BUDGET_MIB.

Crossing the budget is reported as vram_budget_reclaim, always on: a
device reclaiming under budget pressure is one whose next admissions will
reclaim again, and a silent recovery would leave the run before it looking
healthy.

Apply in the reims-vgpu source root:  patch -p1 < this file

diff --git a/crates/reims-vgpu-config/src/lib.rs b/crates/reims-vgpu-config/src/lib.rs
index 6e0c29ea..1e587e18 100644
--- a/crates/reims-vgpu-config/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/reims-vgpu-config/src/lib.rs
@@ -1227,6 +1227,25 @@ counts! {
 /// `crate::runtime::gpu_hang_trail`'s ring covers every submission still in
 /// flight instead of the last half millisecond of a batch.
 pub const BATCH_DRAWS: &str = "REIMS_VGPU_BATCH_DRAWS";
+
+/// **A count, not a switch.** How many MiB the DEVICE_LOCAL image slab may
+/// hold before a new resident admission reclaims recoverable residents,
+/// narrowing the structural budget the engine derives from the heap size.
+/// Read through [`count`], so a value above that device's budget is refused
+/// rather than obeyed.
+///
+/// It exists because "the allocator refuses" is only a bound on hosts whose
+/// driver actually refuses. The registry's live-resident population is bounded
+/// by allocation-pressure recovery (`ResourcePools::reclaim_for_allocation_retry`),
+/// and on the Maleoon 920 / hvgr host that bound never fires: the kernel logs
+/// `GPU_OVER_LIMIT` past ~6 GiB of process GPU pages but `vkAllocateMemory`
+/// keeps succeeding, so a mostly idle desktop grew its registry to 7.2 GiB -
+/// 2 688 residents, half of them evictable by the engine's own predicate -
+/// until the guest panicked behind never-signalling fences. The budget moves
+/// the same lawful reclaim (`recoverable_residents`: not pinned, not sole
+/// copy, not window-held) ahead of the kernel's wall instead of behind a
+/// driver error that never arrives.
+pub const SLAB_BUDGET_MIB: &str = "REIMS_VGPU_SLAB_BUDGET_MIB";
 }
 
